what_would_settle_it:
A redefined KS-0 table built from git log --all, with the subject regex allowing a leading '@ ', and including reverts plus feat/chore commits whose bodies describe a failure. Run it while the squashed PR branches still exist on origin. For each repeat, record:
- an encounter id (a distinct commit on a distinct day or with a different trailer);
- whether a guard or prose entry already existed at the time;
- a cost proxy: an opaque symptom (EAS_BUILD_UNKNOWN_GRADLE_ERROR, a device-only exception, a prod 500) or a self-diagnosing one (a mypy message);
- the tier from the trailer.
Then count only costly, cross-encounter repeats that happened before or despite a guard. Stop at 2 or fewer of 48 or more (upper bound 12.5%, below the 15% break-even). Whether that count reaches 3 is what settles the claim.
